    <name>
      <text>Rationalizing the denominator Q2</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Rationalize the denominator of the following expression by multiplying by the conjugate, giving your answer in its simplest form:</p>
<p>\[ \frac{ {@p@} }{ \sqrt{ {@a@} } + \sqrt{ {@b@} } } \]</p>

<p><strong>Hint</strong>: You can type the answer of: \(\frac{2\left(\sqrt{4}-\sqrt{2}\right)}{2}\) as: <code>(2*(sqrt(4)-sqrt(2)))/2</code></p>

<p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution.</h3>
<p>To rationalize \( \dfrac{ {@p@} }{ \sqrt{ {@a@} } + \sqrt{ {@b@} } } \), multiply top and bottom by the <strong>conjugate</strong> of the denominator, \( \sqrt{ {@a@} } - \sqrt{ {@b@} } \):</p>
<p>\[ \frac{ {@p@} }{ \sqrt{ {@a@} } + \sqrt{ {@b@} } } \times \frac{ \sqrt{ {@a@} } - \sqrt{ {@b@} } }{ \sqrt{ {@a@} } - \sqrt{ {@b@} } } = \frac{ {@p@} (\sqrt{ {@a@} } - \sqrt{ {@b@} }) }{ {@a@} - {@b@} } \]</p>
<p><strong>Key idea:</strong> \( (\sqrt{@a@}+\sqrt{@b@})(\sqrt{@a@}-\sqrt{@b@}) = {@a@} - {@b@} \) a difference of squares which removes both square roots from the denominator in one step.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
